bug_description:
### 🐛 Describe the bug

When a custom device backend relies on CPU fallback for unimplemented operations, `CompositeExplicitAutograd` operations fail to return properly sized tensors. This affects operations like `abs`, `isfinite`, `isinf`, and `isnan`.

assuming we have implemented the minimal set of ops as described: https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/accelerator/operators.html for a privateuse1 out-of-tree backend, and implementing the cpu fallback as described (just a call to `at::native::cpu_fallback(op, stack);`)

```python
# This fails - returns tensor with shape [0]
t = torch.randn(4, 4, device='privateuse1')
result = torch.abs(t)  # result.shape == torch.Size([0])

# But these work correctly
torch.abs(t, out=pre_allocated_tensor)  # Works
t.abs_()  # Works (in-place)
torch.neg(t)  # Works (uses structured_delegate)
```

## Possible Root Cause

### 1. Implementation Pattern

In `pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/UnaryOps.cpp`:

```cpp
// Line 471-480
template <typename OutImpl>
static inline Tensor unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float(const Tensor& self, OutImpl& out_impl) {
  if (self.is_complex()) {
    const auto float_type = c10::toRealValueType(self.scalar_type());
    Tensor result = at::empty_like(self, self.options().dtype(float_type));
    return out_impl(result, self);
  }

  Tensor result = at::empty({0}, self.options());  // Creates placeholder with shape [0]
  return out_impl(result, self);  // Expects out_impl to resize
}

// Line 546-548
Tensor abs(const Tensor& self) {
  return unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float(self, at::abs_out);
}
```

The pattern creates an empty tensor with shape `[0]` as a placeholder, expecting the `.out` variant (`at::abs_out`) to resize it appropriately.

### 2. How the Resize is Expected to Happen

When `out_impl(result, self)` is called with the empty `[0]` shaped tensor, here's the expected resize chain:

1. **`at::abs_out()` is called** (UnaryOps.cpp:543-544):
   ```cpp
   Tensor& abs_out(const Tensor& self, Tensor& result) {
     return unary_op_impl_with_complex_to_float_out(result, self, abs_stub, false);
   }
   ```

2. **Creates TensorIterator** (UnaryOps.cpp:405-408):
   ```cpp
   Tensor& unary_op_impl_out(Tensor& result, const Tensor& self, Stub& stub) {
     auto iter = TensorIterator::unary_op(result, self);  // KEY: This should handle resize
     stub(iter.device_type(), iter);
     return result;
   }
   ```

3. **TensorIterator detects empty tensor and marks for resize** (TensorIterator.cpp:1203-1210):
   ```cpp
   if (!output.defined()) {
     operands_[i].will_resize = true;
   }
   if (output.defined() && !output.sizes().equals(shape_)) {
     if (config.resize_outputs_ && !operands_[i].is_read_write) {
       operands_[i].will_resize = true;  // Marks empty [0] tensor for resize
     }
   }
   ```

4. **TensorIterator allocates/resizes output** (TensorIterator.cpp:574-601):
   ```cpp
   // In allocate_or_resize_outputs()
   if (!op.tensor_base().defined() || op.will_resize) {
     // Calculates correct shape and strides
     set_output_raw_strided(i, tensor_shape, tensor_stride, original_options(op), names_);
   }
   ```

5. **Actual resize happens** (TensorIterator.cpp:1644-1651):
   ```cpp
   if (!op.tensor_base().defined()) {
     // Creates new tensor with correct shape
     op.tensor(c10::MaybeOwned<TensorBase>::owned(at::empty(sizes, options)));
   } else if (op.will_resize) {
     // Resizes existing tensor to correct shape
     at::native::resize_output(op.tensor(), sizes);
   }
   ```

### 3. Why the Resize is Lost

When the operation goes through CPU fallback (`pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/CPUFallback.cpp`):

1. **Input Conversion** (lines 114-147):
   ```cpp
   // Simplified from CPUFallback.cpp
   auto ivalue = (*stack)[idx];
   if (ivalue.isTensor()) {
     auto src_tensor = ivalue.toTensor();
     // Convert to CPU tensor
     (*stack)[idx] = c10::IValue(src_tensor.cpu());
   }
   ```
